    <h2>The Myth That the Can Is What Lasts</h2>

    <p>Walk into any paint store and the whole pitch is the product — this line lasts longer, that one resists fade, upgrade for durability. Paint quality is real, but it's the smallest lever in whether a finish holds. A top-tier exterior paint rolled over chalky, peeling, or dirty siding fails exactly as fast as bargain paint, because adhesion is a property of the surface, not the coating. The myth that a better can buys a better job is convenient for everyone selling paint and expensive for the homeowner who believes it.</p>

    <h2>Prep Is the Job; Paint Is the Last Coat</h2>

    <p>The reality is that a good exterior job is mostly work you can't see in the finished product. The surface gets washed to kill mildew and strip chalk — a real concern in the humid Shoals climate, where mildew grows back on a north wall that never dries. Failing paint gets scraped to sound material, glossy and chalky areas get sanded so the new coat can grip, bare wood gets primed, and gaps get caulked so water can't get behind the film. Only then does color go on, usually two coats. When a job fails in a year, it's almost never the paint — it's that one of those steps got rushed. A painter who leads with the color options and never mentions prep is selling you the last ten percent of the job.</p>

    <h2>What Careful Painters Do on an Old House</h2>

    <p>The historic exteriors in the Wood Avenue and Walnut Street districts in Florence and the Sheffield Residential Historic District need more than fresh color — they need someone who treats the surface as carpentry-plus-paint. That means checking for and repairing rotted trim, glazing failing window putty, and, critically, working lead-safe. Homes this old carry lead in their older paint layers, and a careful crew contains the work and cleans up with HEPA methods instead of sanding lead dust across the yard. The honest admission the quick crews avoid: prepping a hundred-year-old exterior properly is slow, tedious, and where the real money and labor go — which is exactly why the cheap bid skips it.</p>

    <h2>Red Flags in a Paint Quote</h2>

    <p>A quote that's all about brand and color and silent on washing, scraping, and priming is a quote for a short-lived job. A bid dramatically under everyone else's usually means one coat instead of two, or prep trimmed to nothing. And a painter who wants to dry-scrape or power-sand an obviously old exterior without a word about lead is telling you they either don't know or don't care — either one is a reason to keep calling.</p>

    <h2 id="pricing-summary">What Painting Costs Across the Shoals</h2>

    <p>Real ranges, and what moves them:</p>
    <ul class="trades-list-block">
      <li><strong>Interior painting</strong> — about $3 to $6 per square foot of floor area, driven by trim, ceilings, and patching.</li>
      <li><strong>Whole-house exterior</strong> — roughly $4,500 to $12,000, with historic homes at the high end.</li>
      <li><strong>Trim and carpentry repair</strong> — a real line item on old homes, priced by how much wood is failing.</li>
      <li><strong>Single-room repaint</strong> — a common starting job, quick and low-cost relative to a whole exterior.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The subdivision interiors in Muscle Shoals are the higher-volume, faster work; the exterior work on the older Florence and Sheffield homes is the higher-ticket, slower work where prep and repair dominate the bill.</p>

    <h2>The Smart Way to Time an Exterior</h2>

    <p>Exterior paint goes on best in the drier stretches, because coating over damp wood in peak humidity traps moisture and invites peeling. Catching failing paint early — while it's flaking in spots rather than sheeting off — keeps a repaint from turning into a full strip-and-prime. And on an old house, pairing the repaint with the trim repair it already needs saves a second setup, since the ladders and staging are the expensive part to mobilize twice.</p>

